{
  "id": "COMPOUND:22787 - PROTEIN:5867",
  "verbalization": "Khrinone C is implicated in the biological activity against HaCaT cells through a series of molecular interactions. The core mechanism involves Khrinone C targeting the CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, an enzyme that plays a role in sialylation, a process critical for cell surface glycoprotein formation. This enzyme is subsequently targeted by multiple compounds, including 2-Methylheptyl propanoate and (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan, which both have a direct effect on HaCaT cells. The evidence suggests that Khrinone C may exert its biological activity through a pathway that involves the modulation of sialyltransferase activity, potentially influencing cellular processes in HaCaT cells. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Khrinone C in modulating cellular functions through the regulation of sialyltransferase, offering a plausible mechanism for its interaction with HaCaT cells.",
